1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,066
Rotate gradient descent into the SVD basis, with the weights and the targets rotated the same way.

2
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:20,433
Each singular direction obeys its own scalar recurrence, with factor one minus two eta sigma squared.

3
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:29,866
Start at zero and solve it. Direction i reaches the fraction q t of the converged answer, y tilde over sigma.

4
00:00:29,866 --> 00:00:37,466
Plot q t against sigma. After a few steps only large singular values are fit; small ones have barely started.

5
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:44,933
Keep stepping and the curve rises from the right: large singular values converge first, small ones far later.

6
00:00:44,933 --> 00:00:51,533
Compare with ridge. Both hold back weak directions, so stopping early regularizes without a penalty.

7
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:59,100
The curves need not coincide. What they share is the ordering: weak singular directions enter last.

8
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:08,766
This ordering is guaranteed only for eta up to one over two sigma max squared: here sigma is 1.5, eta 0.1.

9
00:01:08,766 --> 00:01:17,833
Up to one over sigma max squared, the largest directions overshoot: q t goes above one, then settles.

10
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:27,433
Now watch four directions, with singular values 3, 1, 0.3 and 0.1, noisy data, and a fixed step size.

11
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:35,066
Training loss only ever falls: given enough steps, gradient descent fits every direction, noise included.

12
00:01:35,066 --> 00:01:42,066
The distance falls, then climbs as noisy weak directions are fit. Best stopping is near 36 steps.

13
00:01:42,066 --> 00:01:49,500
Run to convergence and the error is many times larger. Stopping early trades a little bias for much less noise.

14
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:57,566
In practice, save checkpoints and keep the one with the best validation score, not an arbitrary step count.

15
00:01:57,566 --> 00:02:04,333
Regularization strength, learning rate and stopping time are hyperparameters spanning orders of magnitude.

16
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:13,233
So sweep on a log scale. Evenly spaced values bunch into one decade; one value per power of ten spans it all.

17
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:19,766
Each candidate is trained, then scored on validation data. The test set is kept for the final procedure.

18
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:29,566
A grid grows exponentially: five values per knob is 5, 25, 125 runs, and over fifteen thousand for six knobs.

19
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:36,500
Random search tries new values of every knob on every run, usually a better use of the same budget.

20
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:43,700
With no budget for broad search, borrow settings from a closely related paper: an engineering prior, not proof.

21
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:51,900
Could another learner tune the knobs? Meta-learning can, but it is an outer loop with its own data and cost.

